\id MIC - Open English Translation—Readers’ Version (OET-RV) v0.2.9
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Micah
\toc1 Micah
\toc2 Micah
\mt1 Micah
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\p
\v 1 This is Yahweh’s message that came to Micah (from Morashet) in the days of Judah’s kings Jotham, Ahaz, and Hezekiah, that he saw concerning Samaria and Jerusalem.
\s1 Samaria and Jerusalem’s mourning
\s4 Both kingdoms
\q1
\v 2 Listen, all you peoples.
\q1 Let the world and everything in it pay attention.
\q1 Let my master Yahweh be against you as a witness,
\q1 my master from his sacred temple
\q1
\v 3 because wow, Yahweh comes out of his place.
\q1 He will come down and tread on the high places\f + \fr 1:3 \ft Possibly referring to the pagan temples which were often constructed on hilltops.\f* of the earth.
\q1
\v 4 The mountains will melt under him.
\q1 The valleys will break apart,
\q1 like wax that’s near a fire,
\q1 like waters that are poured out from a steep place.
\b
\q1
\v 5 All that is because of Jacob’s disobedience,
\q1 and because of the sins of the Israelite people.
\q1 What was Jacob’s transgression? Isn’t it Samaria?
\q1 What is Judah’s high place? Isn’t it Jerusalem?
\q1
\v 6 “I will make Samaria a heap of ruins in the countryside—
\q1 a place for planting vineyards.
\q1 Then I’ll pour the stones \add from her buildings\add* down into the valley
\q1 and I’ll uncover her foundations.
\q1
\v 7 All her carved statues will be smashed into pieces,
\q1 and all her wages will be burnt.
\q1 \add Yes,\add* I’ll totally destroy all her idols.
\q1 Since she used prostitutes’ wages to pay for them,
\q1 the \add crushed pieces of\add* idols will become prostitutes’ wages again.”
\p
\v 8 Because of that, I’ll mourn and wail.
\q1 I’ll go barefoot and undressed.
\q1 I’ll wail like the jackals
\q1 and mourn like owls
\q1
\v 9 because her wound is incurable.
\q1 Yes, it’s come to Judah.
\q1 It’s reached the gate of my people—reached Jerusalem.
\s1 War is coming to Jerusalem
\s4 Southern kingdom
\q1
\v 10 Don’t tell about it in Gath.
\q1 Don’t weep at all.
\q1 I roll myself in the dust at Beyt-Le’afrah \add (the house of dust)\add*.
\q1
\v 11 Pass by undressed and humiliated, inhabitants of Shafir.
\q1 Don’t come out, inhabitants of Tsa’anan.
\q1 Beyt-Haetsel mourns—its place to stand is taken from you
\q1
\v 12 because the inhabitants of Marot wait anxiously for good,
\q1 because disaster has come down from Yahweh to Jerusalem’s gates.
\q1
\v 13 Harness the chariot to the team of horses, inhabitants of Lakish.
\q1 She was the beginning of sin for the daughter of Zion,
\q1 because Israel’s transgressions were \add first\add* found in you.
\q1
\v 14 So you’ll give parting gifts to Moreshet-Gath.
\q1 The town of Akziv will deceive Israel’s kings.
\b
\q1
\v 15 I will again bring a conqueror to you, inhabitants of Mareshah.
\q1 Israel’s best will come to Adulam.
\q1
\v 16 Shave your head
\q1 and cut off your hair
\q1 for the children who you delight in.
\q1 Make yourselves as bald as eagles,
\q1 because they’ll be taken from you \add and\add* into exile.
\c 2
\s1 Punishing the oppressors
\q1
\v 1 Woe to those who plan to do wicked things—
\q1 to those who do evil on their beds.
\q1 They do it in the morning light
\q1 because they make use of their power.
\q1
\v 2 They see fields that they’d like and seize them,
\q1 and houses and they \add just\add* take them.
\q1 They’ll oppress a man and his household—
\q1 \add even\add* taking property handed down in the family.
\b
\q1
\v 3 Therefore Yahweh says,
\q1 “Listen, I’m planning to bring disaster against this clan,
\q1 which you won’t be able to avoid.
\q1 You won’t walk \add so\add* proudly,
\q1 because it’ll be a terrible time \add for you all\add*.
\q1
\v 4 In that day someone will sing a song against you all.
\q1 and mourn with a song of wailing,
\q1 and say, ‘We’re completely ruined.
\q1 Yahweh changes my people’s territory.
\q1 How can he remove the land from me?
\q1 He divides our fields up to give to traitors.’ ”
\b
\q1
\v 5 Therefore, there’ll be no one in Yahweh’s assembly
\q1 who’ll \add have authority\add* to measure you out any more land.
\b
\q1
\v 6 “Don’t tell us that,”
\q1 they tell us!
\q1 “They shouldn’t preach those things—
\q1 \add that sort of\add* disgrace will never happen to us.”
\q1
\v 7 Do Jacob’s descendants \add really\add* ask,
\q1 “Is Yahweh’s spirit impatient?
\q1 Are these his actions?”
\q1 Don’t my words do good to anyone who obeys them?
\b
\q1
\v 8 Lately my people have risen up \add against me\add* like an enemy.
\q1 You strip the rich robe off those who are innocently going past,
\q1 like a soldier on the battlefield might have done to his enemy.
\q1
\v 9 You’ve driven our widows out of their pleasant houses.
\q1 You’ve forever deprived their young children of what I’d blessed them with.
\q1
\v 10 Get ready and leave because this isn’t a place for resting.
\q1 It’s about to be completely destroyed because of its disobedience.
\b
\q1
\v 11 If someone comes to you full of talk and lies and says,
\q1 “I’ll prophesy to you about \add enjoying\add* wine and strong drink,”
\q1 he’d be considered to be a prophet for this people.
\q1
\v 12 I will certainly gather all of you, Jacob.
\q1 I’ll definitely assemble the remnant of Israel.
\q1 I’ll bring them together like sheep in a pen—
\q1 like a flock in the middle of their pasture.
\q1 Noisy because there’ll be so many people.
\b
\q1
\v 13 Someone will go in front of them to break open the way.
\q1 They’ll force open the gate and go out.
\q1 Their king will pass on ahead of them.
\q1 Yahweh will be at their head.
\c 3
\s1 Scolding Israel’s leaders and prophets
\s4 Both kingdoms
\q1
\v 1 Then I said, “Now listen,
\q1 you leaders of Jacob,
\q1 and rulers of the house of Israel:
\q1 Isn’t it your responsibility to understand justice?
\q1
\v 2 Those who hate good
\q1 and love evil,
\q1 those who tear their skin off them
\q1 and their flesh from their bones,
\q1
\v 3 those who eat the flesh of my people,
\q1 and their skin that they’ve stripped off them,
\q1 they break their bones,
\q1 and chop them into pieces,
\q1 just like \add soup\add* in a pot—
\q1 like meat in a cauldron.
\q1
\v 4 Then they’ll call out to Yahweh \add for help\add*,
\q1 ^but he won’t answer them.
\q1 He’ll hide himself from them at that time,
\q1 because they’ve done evil things.”
\b
\q1
\v 5 So Yahweh speaks \add out\add* against the ‘prophets’ who lead his people astray:
\q1 “They lie through their teeth and declare, ‘Peace.’
\q1 They’re fighting against the one who never put a single word into their mouths.
\q1
\v 6 Therefore, it’ll be night for you \add prophets\add*, without any visions.
\q1 It will be dark for you all, without any prophesying.
\q1 The sun will go down on the ‘prophets’,
\q1 and the day will be black for them.
\q1
\v 7 The tellers of the future will be put to shame,
\q1 and the diviners will be disgraced.
\q1 They will cover their lips, all of them,
\q1 because there’ll be no answer from God.”
\b
\q1
\v 8 But I’m full of power from Yahweh’s spirit,
\q1 and full of justice and strength,
\q1 to declare to Jacob his disobedience,
\q1 and to Israel his sin.
\q1
\v 9 Now listen to this,
\q1 you leaders of the house of Jacob,
\q1 and rulers of the house of Israel,
\q1 you all who detest justice,
\q1 and who take everything that’s right and make it crooked.
\q1
\v 10 You build Zion with blood
\q1 and Jerusalem with evil.
\q1
\v 11 Her leaders judge for a bribe,
\q1 her priests teach for a price,
\q1 and her prophets do divination for money.
\q1 ^Yet they \add claim to\add* depend on Yahweh, and they say,
\q1 “Isn’t Yahweh right here with us?
\q1 Nothing bad can happen to us.”
\b
\q1
\v 12 Therefore, because of you all,
\q1 Zion will become a ploughed field,
\q1 Jerusalem will become a heap of rubble,
\q1 and the temple hill will become covered with scrub.
\c 4
\s1 Yahweh’s mountain
\s4 Both kingdoms
\r (Isa. 2:2-4)
\q1
\v 1 In the last days it’ll come about
\q1 that the mountain where Yahweh’s residence is
\q1 will become more important than \add other\add* mountains.
\q1 Then it’ll be lifted above the hills,
\q1 and peoples will stream to it.
\q1
\v 2 Many nations will go and say,
\q1 “Come, let’s go up to Yahweh’s mountain,
\q1 to the house of the Jacob’s god
\q1 →so that he will teach us his ways,
\q1 and we will walk on his paths.”
\q1 Because the law will go out from Zion,
\q1 and Yahweh’s message from Jerusalem.
\b
\q1
\v 3 He’ll judge among many peoples
\q1 and will arbitrate for strong countries far away.
\q1 They’ll beat their swords into plough blades
\q1 and their spears into pruning hooks.
\q1 Nations won’t attack \add other\add* nations,
\q1 nor will they train for war any longer.
\q1
\v 4 Then every person will \add be able to\add* sit under their grapevine
\q1 and under their fig tree.
\q1 No one will make them afraid,
\q1 because Commander-in-chief Yahweh has spoken.
\b
\q1
\v 5 All various people groups follow their own god,
\q1 but we will obey our god Yahweh’s \add instructions\add*
\q1 forever and ever.
\s1 Israel will return from exile
\s4 Both kingdoms
\q1
\v 6 Yahweh declares: “At that time, I’ll gather the lame people
\q1 and assemble those who were scattered—those who I’ve done harm to.
\q1
\v 7 And I will turn those lame people into a \add growing\add* group,
\q1 and those scattered ones into a strong nation,
\q1 and Yahweh will reign over them on Mt. Zion,
\q1 from now and until forever.
\b
\q1
\v 8 And you, watchtower for the flock,
\q1 hill of the daughter of Zion—
\q1 to you it will come—your former dominion will arrive—
\q1 the kingdom that belongs to the daughter of Jerusalem.
\q1
\v 9 Now, why do you shout so loudly?
\q1 Is it because you don’t have a king?
\q1 Is it because your advisor passed away
\q1 that pain’s gripping you like that of a woman in labour.
\q1
\v 10 Be in pain and labour daughter of Zion,
\q1 like a woman giving birth.
\q1 For now, you need to go out of the city
\q1 and live in the countryside.
\q1 Then you’ll go to Babylon and be rescued from there.
\q1 There Yahweh will redeem you from your enemies’ control.
\q1
\v 11 Now many nations have assembled against you.
\q1 They say, ‘Let her be overrun.
\q1 Let our eyes gloat over Zion’s \add fall\add*.’
\q1
\v 12 But those enemies don’t know Yahweh’s thoughts,
\q1 and they don’t understand his plans,
\q1 because he’s gathered them \add up\add* like bundles of grain ready \add to be broken on\add* the threshing floor.
\b
\q1
\v 13 Get going and thresh, daughter of Zion,
\q1 because I’ll make your horn into iron,
\q1 and I’ll make your hooves into bronze.
\q1 And you’ll break many peoples into pieces
\q1 and you’ll \add confiscate\add* their unjust earnings to devote to Yahweh—
\q1 their wealth will be for the master of the whole world.”
\c 5
\q1
\v 1 Now come together in battle ranks, daughter of soldiers.
\q1 A siege has been set up against us.
\q1 They strike Israel’s judge on the cheek with a rod.
\s1 God promises a ruler from Bethlehem
\s4 Southern kingdom
\q1
\v 2 But you, Bethlehem Efratah,
\q1 even though you’re insignificant among Judah’s clans,
\q1 out of you, one will come to me to rule in Israel,
\q1 whose beginning is from ancient times—
\q1 from everlasting.
\b
\q1
\v 3 Therefore God will hand them over \add to their enemies\add*,
\q1 until the time when the woman in labour gives birth,
\q1 and the rest of his brothers return to the people of Israel.
\q1
\v 4 He’ll stand and shepherd his flock with Yahweh’s strength,
\q1 in the majesty of the name of his god Yahweh.
\q1 They’ll remain, because then he will be famous to the ends of the earth.
\q2
\v 5 That one will be our peace.
\q1 When the Assyrians come into our land
\q1 and when they march against our fortresses,
\q1 →then we’ll raise seven shepherds against them
\q1 and eight leaders of men.
\s1 God’s salvation and his vengeance
\q1
\v 6 They’ll shepherd Assyria with the sword—
\q1 that country founded by Nimrod in its entrances.
\q1 He’ll rescue us from the Assyrians,
\q1 when they come into our land,
\q1 when they march inside our borders.
\b
\q1
\v 7 The remnant of Jacob will end up among many peoples,
\q1 like dew from Yahweh,
\q1 like showers on the grass,
\q1 that don’t need help from any person,
\q1 and they don’t rely on human intervention.
\q1
\v 8 The remnant of Jacob will be among the nations,
\q1 among many peoples,
\q1 like a lion among the animals of the forest,
\q1 like a young lion among the flocks of sheep.
\q1 As he passes through them,
\q1 he’ll trample over them and tear them to pieces,
\q1 and there’ll be no one to save them.
\q1
\v 9 You’ll use your power against your enemies,
\q1 and they’ll be destroyed.
\b
\q1
\v 10 Yahweh declares: “At that time,
\q1 I’ll wipe out your horses
\q1 and demolish your chariots.
\q1
\v 11 I’ll destroy the cities in your country
\q1 and tear down all your fortresses.
\q1
\v 12 I’ll destroy the witchcraft that you practise,
\q1 and you’ll no longer have any diviners.
\q1
\v 13 I’ll destroy your carved statues,
\q1 and the \add engraved\add* \add stone\add* pillars from among you.
\q1 You’ll no longer worship the workmanship of your hands.
\q1
\v 14 I’ll topple your Asherah poles where you worship,
\q1 and I’ll destroy your cities.
\q1
\v 15 In my anger, I’ll execute vengeance
\q1 on the nations that haven’t obeyed.
\c 6
\s1 Yahweh’s challenge to Israel
\s4 Full kingdom
\q1
\v 1 Now listen to what Yahweh says,
\q1 “Take action, present your case to the mountains.
\q1 and let the hills hear your voice.
\q1
\v 2 Listen to Yahweh’s lawsuit, you mountains,
\q1 and you enduring foundations of the earth,
\q1 because Yahweh has a lawsuit with his people,
\q1 and he’ll present evidence against Israel.”
\q1
\v 3 “My people, what have I done to you?
\q1 How have I worn you down?
\q1 Present your case against me.
\q1
\v 4 I rescued you out of slavery
\q1 and brought you out of Egypt.
\q1 I sent Moses, Aaron, and Miriam to you.
\q1
\v 5 My people, remember what Moab’s King Balak devised,
\q1 and how Beor’s son Balaam answered that king
\q1 as you went from Shitim to Gilgal.
\q1 Think about that so you can know Yahweh’s righteous actions.”
\s1 The desire of Master God
\q1
\v 6 What should I bring to Yahweh,
\q1 as I bow down to the high God?
\q1 Should I come to him with burnt offerings,
\q1 with year-old calves?
\q1
\v 7 Will Yahweh be pleased with thousands of rams,
\q1 or with ten thousand rivers of oil?
\q1 Should I sacrifice my oldest child \add to pay\add* for my disobedience—
\q1 my biological offspring \add to pay\add* for my own sin?
\q1
\v 8 He has told humanity what is good,
\q1 and what Yahweh requires from you:
\q1 Act justly,
\q1 love kindness,
\q1 and walk humbly with your god.
\b
\q1
\v 9 Yahweh is making an announcement to the city—
\q1 even now wisdom is calling out to you:
\q1 “Pay attention to the rod \add of punishment\add*,
\q1 and to the one who has put it in place.
\q1
\v 10 There’s dishonest wealth in wicked people’s houses,
\q1 along with hated false \add weights and\add* measures.
\q1
\v 11 Should I consider people to be innocent if they use fraudulent scales
\q1 or a bag of deceptive weights?
\q1
\v 12 The city’s rich men are full of violence,
\q1 the inhabitants have spoken lies,
\q1 and you can’t believe a word that they say.
\q1
\v 13 Therefore I’ll strike you with a terrible blow,
\q1 and I’ll make you into a desolate country because of your sins.
\q1
\v 14 You’ll eat but not be satisfied—your emptiness will remain inside you.
\q1 You’ll store goods away but not be able to save them, and what you do save I will be taken by force.
\q1
\v 15 You’ll sow, but not harvest.
\q1 You’ll tread the olives, but not anoint yourselves with their oil.
\q1 You’ll press grapes, but not drink the wine.
\q1
\v 16 \add As a city,\add* you’ve kept the regulations made by \add King\add* Omri,
\q1 and behaved similarly to Ahab’s \add evil\add* descendants.
\q1 You walk by their advice, so I’ll make you into a ruin.
\q1 People will hiss at your inhabitants,
\q1 and my people will mock you.”
\c 7
\s1 Dysfunctional society
\q1
\v 1 I’m miserable because I’ve become like the gathering of left-over fruit,
\q1 and like the grapes that have already been gleaned.
\q1 There’s no grape clusters to eat,
\q1 \add and\add* none of the ripe early figs that I really felt like.
\q1
\v 2 The faithful people have disappeared from the land.
\q1 There is no godly person among all mankind.
\q1 They all lie in wait to shed blood—
\q1 each one hunts his own fellow countryman with a net.
\q1
\v 3 They’re very good at doing harm:
\q1 the leader asks for money,
\q1 the judge is ready for bribes,
\q1 and the powerful man is telling others what he wants to obtain,
\q1 →and so they scheme together.
\q1
\v 4 The best of them is like a thorn-bush.
\q1 The most upright is worse than a prickly hedge.
\b
\q1 It’s the day foretold by your watchmen—
\q1 the day of your punishment.
\q1 Now is the time of their confusion.
\q1
\v 5 Don’t trust any neighbour.
\q1 Put no confidence in any friend.
\q1 Be careful about what you say
\q1 even to the woman who lies in your arms,
\q1
\v 6 because sons dishonour their fathers,
\q1 daughters rise up against their mothers,
\q1 and daughters-in-law against their mothers-in-law.
\q1 A person’s enemies are the people in their own household.
\b
\q1
\v 7 But as for me, I’ll look out for Yahweh.
\q1 I’ll wait for the god who saves me.
\q1 My god will hear me.
\s1 The saving/explaining of Master God
\q1
\v 8 Don’t be happy about \add what happened to\add* me, my enemy.
\q1 After I fall, I’ll get up and stand \add again\add*.
\q1 When I sit in darkness, Yahweh will be a light for me.
\q1
\v 9 Because I sinned against Yahweh, I’ll endure his rage
\q1 until he pleads my cause \add on my behalf\add*, and executes judgement for me.
\q1 He’ll bring me to the light, and I’ll see him rescue me in his justice.
\q1
\v 10 Then my enemy will see it,
\q1 and shame will cover the one who said to me,
\q1 “Where is Yahweh your god?”
\q1 My eyes will look at her;
\q1 she’ll be trampled down like the mud in the streets.
\b
\q1
\v 11 The time will come for rebuilding your walls.
\q1 On that day your boundaries will be greatly expanded.
\q1
\v 12 On that day \add your people\add* will come to you,
\q1 from Assyria and the cities in Egypt,
\q1 from Egypt to the \add Euphrates\add* river,
\q1 from sea to sea,
\q1 and from mountain to mountain.
\q1
\v 13 But the land will be desolate
\q1 because of the people who are living there,
\q1 because of the fruit of their actions.
\s1 God’s mercy towards Israel
\q1
\v 14 Shepherd your people with your staff—
\q1 the flock of your inheritance.
\q1 They live alone in a forest,
\q1 surrounded by pastureland.
\q1 Let them graze in Bashan and Gilead as in the old days.
\p
\v 15 Just like in the days when you were rescued out of Egypt,
\q1 I’ll display miracles to them.
\q1
\v 16 The nations will see and be ashamed of all their power.
\q1 They’ll put their hands over their mouths—
\q1 \add and act like\add* their ears are deaf.
\q1
\v 17 They’ll lick the dust like a snake—
\q1 like creatures that crawl on the earth.
\q1 They’ll be trembling as they come out of their fortresses.
\q1 They’ll come with fear to you, our god Yahweh,
\q1 and they’ll be afraid because of you.
\b
\q1
\v 18 What other God is there like you, who takes away sin?
\q1 Who forgives the disobedience of the remnant of his inherited people?
\q1 He doesn’t stay angry forever,
\q1 because he happily demonstrates his loyal commitment.
\q1
\v 19 You’ll have compassion on us \add yet\add* again.
\q1 You’ll wipe away our disobedience.
\q1 You will throw all our sins into the deepest parts of the sea.
\q1
\v 20 You’ll give truth to Jacob
\q1 and loyal commitment to Abraham,
\q1 as you promised to our ancestors long ago.
